# Why is my Laravel 11 exception handler not working?

In Laravel 11, the architecture for configuring the application has shifted significantly from the traditional `app/Http/Kernel.php` approach to a more declarative setup within `bootstrap/app.php`. This change introduces specific nuances when attempting to customize how exceptions like `AuthorizationException` are rendered. If you find yourself seeing a standard error page instead of your custom redirect logic, it is usually not a bug in the framework but rather a mismatch between the exception type thrown and the handler signature you have defined.

The core issue lies in how Laravel 11 differentiates between HTTP exceptions and application exceptions. The `AuthorizationException` class extends `\Illuminate\Auth\Access\AuthorizationException`, which ultimately inherits from `Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\Exception\UnauthorizedHttpException`. However, there is a distinct category of exceptions handled by the `$exceptions->render()` closure that you might be expecting to catch.

When you define your custom handler inside `bootstrap/app.php` using the fluent interface, the closure passed to `$exceptions->render()` is specifically designed for `Throwable` instances that are not HTTP Exceptions or specific known classes unless explicitly listed. More critically, in many Laravel 11 configurations, the global exception handler logic expects a broader scope than just catching one specific class like `AuthorizationException`.

The most common reason this fails is that `AuthorizationException` is often converted into an HTTP response automatically by the framework before it reaches your custom render closure if no specific configuration exists for it. However, in Laravel 11's new bootstrap file, you must explicitly tell the exception handler how to treat `AuthorizationException`.

To fix this, you need to ensure your exception handler logic is robust enough to intercept the specific HTTP status code associated with authorization failures or explicitly target the exception class. Here is a more direct approach to configuring the `Exceptions` object in `bootstrap/app.php`:

```php
use Illuminate\Auth\Access\AuthorizationException;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Application;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Configuration\Exceptions;

return Application::configure(basePath: dirname(__DIR__))
    ->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware) {
        // ... middleware configuration
    })
    ->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions) {
        // Explicitly handle AuthorizationException
        $exceptions->render(function (AuthorizationException $e, Request $request) {
            return redirect()
                ->route('dashboard')
                ->withErrors($e->getMessage());
        });

        // If the above does not work, consider handling by HTTP status code
        // because Laravel sometimes normalizes these exceptions to 403 responses first.
        $exceptions->render(function ($exception, Request $request) {
            if ($exception instanceof \Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\Exception\UnauthorizedHttpException) {
                return redirect()->route('dashboard')->withErrors($exception->getMessage());
            }

            // Fallback for other exceptions
            if ($exception instanceof AuthorizationException) {
                 return redirect()->route('dashboard')->withErrors($exception->getMessage());
            }

            // Default rendering logic for other errors
            return $exception->reportAndDisplay($request);
        });
    })
    ->create();
```

By adding a check for the underlying `UnauthorizedHttpException` or ensuring the closure handles the broad `Throwable` interface with specific conditions, you guarantee that the framework doesn't bypass your logic. The previous screenshot from the issue indicates that the custom redirect is not firing, which suggests the exception is being caught by the default Symfony error handling layer before Laravel's new bootstrap handler has a chance to render it as a redirect.

Another potential pitfall involves the order of operations in `bootstrap/app.php`. If you have defined routes or middleware that implicitly resolve these exceptions before they hit the global handler, your custom logic might be skipped. Ensure that the exception is indeed bubbling up to the top level of the application lifecycle.

Finally, verify that your routing definitions for the dashboard are accessible and that the `withErrors` method is being handled correctly by your Blade templates or API response formatters. If you are using `barryvdh/laravel-debugbar`, inspect the request lifecycle in the debug bar to see exactly where the exception is caught. You will likely find that without an explicit check for the HTTP exception subclass, Laravel 11 defaults to rendering the HTML error page for unauthorized access unless instructed otherwise via the `render` closure.

For deeper insights into managing application flow and exceptions in modern Laravel versions, you might want to explore resources like <https://laravelcompany.com> which often covers architectural shifts in detail.
